<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=artemisinin">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artemisinin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as qinghaosu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artemisinin is a drug against malaria caused by Plasmodium falciparum, discovered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1972 by Tu Youyou in the leaves of sweet wormwood (Artemisia annua). It came out of Project 523, a secret military programme of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> set up in 1967 that screened a list of nearly 2,000 traditional Chinese medicines; Tu said she was guided by a handbook of 340 CE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ge-hong">Ge Hong</a>. Artemisinin-based combination therapies have become the standard treatment for malaria worldwide, and Tu shared the 2015 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physiology or Medicine for the discovery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artemisinin.jpg" alt="Artemisinin" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jorge Ferreira · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artemisia_annua.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Sweet wormwood was an old remedy. In 1596 Li Shizhen recommended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> made from it specifically for the symptoms of malaria in his Compendium of Materia Medica. The modern search began in 1967, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> set up Project 523, a plant screening programme. According to the account given, it and its early clinical work were ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> at the request of North Vietnamese leaders, to help their malaria-ridden army. In the course of this research Tu Youyou found artemisinin in the leaves of Artemisia annua in 1972.</p><p>Named qinghaosu, or compound of green-blue wormwood, it was one of many candidates tested from a list of nearly 2,000 traditional medicines. Tu also found that a low-temperature extraction could isolate an effective substance, and she says she was influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ge-hong">Ge Hong</a>'s instruction of 340 CE to steep the herb in cold water. Her team's results were published in the Chinese Medical Journal in 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Artemisinin and its derivatives are sesquiterpene lactones containing an unusual peroxide bridge, an endoperoxide ring responsible for their antimalarial action, and few other natural compounds have one. Because artemisinin itself is poorly soluble and poorly absorbed, semisynthetic derivatives were made: those of dihydroartemisinin from 1976, and artesunate, arteether and artemether in 1986. Project 523 also developed drugs to be used in combination with it, including lumefantrine, piperaquine and pyronaridine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the late 1990s Novartis filed a Chinese patent for the combination of artemether and lumefantrine, giving the first artemisinin-based combination therapy, Coartem, at reduced prices to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-health-organization">World Health Organization</a>. In 2006 the WHO called for an immediate halt to single-drug artemisinin preparations, to lessen the risk of resistance, and clinical evidence of resistance in south-east Asia was first reported in 2008. Also in 2006, a team at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, Berkeley reported yeast engineered to produce artemisinic acid, the basis of a semisynthetic route that Sanofi developed at Garessio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>; the WHO prequalified its artesunate on 8 May 2013.</p><p>Tu received the Lasker-DeBakey Clinical Medical Research Award in 2011, and on 5 October 2015 half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physiology or Medicine. The other half went to William C. Campbell and Satoshi Ōmura for avermectin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Artemisinin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
